{"ts": 1789771293.262379, "kind": "assistant_text", "text": "The sign structure is coherent and consistent: features 8, 25, 0 long and 10 short each contribute positively in every pairing, which is reassuring \u2014 this looks like genuine structure rather than a lucky sign flip.\n\nI'll go with **{8 long, 25 long, 10 short}** at IS 1.118.\n\nNow the key question is what to predict for OOS. I should **not** predict 1.118. Two distinct effects will pull the OOS value down:\n\n**1. Selection bias.** I evaluated ~60 specs and am submitting the maximum. The bar of 1.020 is the search-adjusted threshold, and my 1.118 only modestly exceeds it \u2014 meaning most of my apparent edge over a \"typical good spec\" is plausibly selection noise. My top ~10 specs span 1.04\u20131.12, a spread consistent with pure noise, so the true quality of the one I picked is likely near the middle of that cluster, not its top.\n\n**2. Estimation noise.** With n=5000, the SE of an annualized Sharpe is \u2248\u221a(1/20) \u2248 0.22 per spec. Picking the max of many correlated draws inflates the estimate by roughly a fraction of that SE.\n\nShrinking for both, I estimate the true Sharpe near **0.80**, with genuine uncertainty \u2014 OOS could plausibly land anywhere from ~0.4 to ~1.15. I'll set sd = 0.28, wide enough to reflect that I cannot distinguish my pick from its near-neighbors."}
