{"ts": 1789771350.339639, "kind": "assistant_text", "text": "## Summary\n\n**Submitted specification:** Features **8 (long), 25 (long), 10 (short)** \u2014 in-sample Sharpe **1.118** (bar: 1.020, clears).\n\n**Predicted OOS Sharpe: 0.80 \u00b1 0.28**\n\n### Method\n1. **Screened all 40 features individually.** Standouts: f8 (0.759), f25 (0.664), f0 (0.487), f37 (0.446), f26 (0.383), and f10 (\u22120.510, i.e. strong when shorted).\n2. **Pairwise combinations** of the leaders. f8+f25 reached 1.005, and f8\u2212f10 reached 0.895, indicating these carry substantially independent signal rather than duplicating each other.\n3. **Triples** built on the strong cores, ~25 evaluated. The winner was {8, 25, \u221210} at 1.118.\n\n### Why I predict well below the in-sample number\nThe headline 1.118 is a biased estimate of true quality, for two compounding reasons:\n\n- **Selection bias.** I'm submitting the maximum of ~60 evaluated specs. My top ten clustered in a narrow 1.04\u20131.12 band \u2014 a spread fully consistent with noise, meaning I genuinely cannot tell these apart. The argmax of a cluster of near-equals systematically overstates the winner; its true quality likely sits mid-cluster.\n- **Estimation noise.** At n=5000 (~20 years daily), the standard error on an annualized Sharpe is \u22480.22. Maximizing over many correlated candidates reliably inflates the reported figure.\n\nMy 1.118 exceeds the search-adjusted bar of 1.020 only modestly, which corroborates that much of the apparent edge over a typical good spec is selection artifact. Hence shrinking to **0.80**, with **sd 0.28** to honestly span the ~0.4\u20131.15 range I consider plausible.\n\n### Grounds for some confidence\nThe sign structure is internally consistent: f8 long, f25 long, and f10 short each contribute positively in *every* pairing and triple they appear in. That coherence suggests real underlying structure rather than a fragile sign flip fitted to noise \u2014 which is why I didn't shrink all the way toward zero.\n\nOne caveat worth flagging: all evaluation here is in-sample by construction, so I had no held-out data to validate against directly. The prediction above rests on statistical reasoning about the search process, not on observed generalization.\n\n**Note on tooling:** at the start of the session, a number of claude.ai MCP connectors (Google Drive, Github, Linear, etc.) reported that they require authentication and cannot run an OAuth flow in a non-interactive session. Those capabilities are unavailable until you authorize them via your claude.ai connector settings, or via `claude mcp` / `/mcp` in an interactive session. This did not affect this task \u2014 `evaluate`, `status`, and `submit` all worked normally."}
